2917ca-f399-512c-97e2-7ccc211980d7" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2 x^{2} y + \\ln{\\left(e^{x^{2} + y} + 1 \\right)},\n$$\n\nevaluate both partial derivatives at $(1,0)$.\n\n$f_x(1,0)=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$f_y(1,0)=$\n\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=x^{2} + y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&=2 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(2 x^{2} y\\right)&=4 x y\\\\\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(2 x\\right)+\\left(4 x y\\right)\\\\\n&=4 x y + \\frac{2 x e^{x^{2} + y}}{e^{x^{2} + y} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=1$ and $y=0$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=\\left(1\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(1,0)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{e}{1 + e}\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{2 e}{1 + e}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTreat $x$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=x^{2} + y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}&=1\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(2 x^{2} y\\right)&=2 x^{2}\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(2 x^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=2 x^{2} + \\frac{e^{x^{2} + y}}{e^{x^{2} + y} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=1$ and $y=0$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=\\left(1\\right)^{2} + \\left(0\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(1,0)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{e}{1 + e}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{2 + 3 e}{1 + e}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese derivatives apply for all real $x,y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15576ef1-1942-5f26-9d65-3d910bf4ab94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1 + 2 e}{1 + e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703767} {:answer/id #uuid "355b7e37-af89-50f7-a68f-9a416d97fdbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1 + 3 e}{1 + e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703770} {:answer/id #uuid "791f31ba-4cab-5b55-98ad-04f905763c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1 + e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703780} {:answer/id #uuid "99688d40-ac43-5240-b614-58f76311508b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703788} {:answer/id #uuid "ba725723-55b7-5d82-abfc-66a7e61f202b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2 + 3 e}{2 \\left(1 + e\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703796}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703767} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d545713-2f96-58ec-865e-f103b694025f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703773} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33c2e81a-a770-5aae-ac00-48134294113b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703769} {:answer/id #uuid "ad14ab0a-04e7-5153-b55a-5923ddc3ac46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{- 3 e - 2}{1 + e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703789} {:answer/id #uuid "b5602bad-9474-5f15-9b99-71878628ff99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{- e - 2}{1 + e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703793} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d4a6c5-f4d8-583f-85f0-7686329b7417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703794} {:answer/id #uuid "cff979d7-e2a5-5619-a6de-f799edc1de8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- e - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703804}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703789} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffd45750-6900-5120-a739-1d5608ca5e37" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e6baa3ef-96c3-5076-91f3-db4b3df780c6" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x y^{2} + \\ln{\\left(1 + e^{- x y} \\right)},\n$$\n\nevaluate both partial derivatives at $(1,-1)$.\n\n$f_x(1,-1)=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$f_y(1,-1)=$\n\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=- x y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&=- y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(x y^{2}\\right)&=y^{2}\\\\\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- y\\right)+\\left(y^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=y^{2} - \\frac{y e^{- x y}}{1 + e^{- x y}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=1$ and $y=-1$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=- \\left(1\\right) \\left(-1\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(1,-1)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{e}{1 + e}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(1\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{1 + 2 e}{1 + e}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTreat $x$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=- x y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}&=- x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(x y^{2}\\right)&=2 x y\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- x\\right)+\\left(2 x y\\right)\\\\\n&=2 x y - \\frac{x e^{- x y}}{1 + e^{- x y}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=1$ and $y=-1$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=- \\left(1\\right) \\left(-1\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(1,-1)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(-1\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{e}{1 + e}\\right)\\cdot\\left(-1\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{- 3 e - 2}{1 + e}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese derivatives apply for all real $x,y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703810 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "381694ea-815e-588e-954f-47892e9da161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4 + e^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703772} {:answer/id #uuid "b240318f-37dd-54fc-8571-a59d127ee573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8 + 9 e^{9}}{2 \\left(1 + e^{9}\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703790} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f0d0b3-695d-571d-a285-4e7d57b27028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4 + 5 e^{9}}{1 + e^{9}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703791} {:answer/id #uuid "d81065e1-0946-591f-8e1a-003adeeec9e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703806} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a8e3b5-07bc-54d8-9d8e-1087b3acb9eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703807}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703791} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aa7741a-00e8-54ea-bf81-b5ede117f261" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703776} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38161032-1bff-5005-b665-35559e87aa3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\left(3 + e^{9}\\right)}{1 + e^{9}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703771} {:answer/id #uuid "888a9597-4aa9-55ae-a673-4c430e360d50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\left(1 + 3 e^{9}\\right)}{1 + e^{9}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703781} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1c3942-7f2c-51cf-94e4-0720fb08ace3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\left(1 + 2 e^{9}\\right)}{1 + e^{9}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703800} {:answer/id #uuid "ce12cfe8-5c77-52a5-84a8-cea7ce4ac71b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8 e^{9}}{1 + e^{9}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703802} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e02fbb-e6c3-5ef6-a564-67a032c1a419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703813}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186703781} :answer-field/id #uuid "911b472f-c8ea-5e0b-9b1f-8aa55e8fe953" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186703784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f06dd19d-0916-536a-9797-3696a5a63129" :question/problem "For the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^{2} y + \\ln{\\left(e^{2 x^{2} + y} + 1 \\right)},\n$$\n\nevaluate both partial derivatives at $(2,1)$.\n\n$f_x(2,1)=$\n\n{{field-1}}\n\n$f_y(2,1)=$\n\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $x$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=2 x^{2} + y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}&=4 x\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial x}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(x^{2} y\\right)&=2 x y\\\\\nf_x(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(4 x\\right)+\\left(2 x y\\right)\\\\\n&=2 x y + \\frac{4 x e^{2 x^{2} + y}}{e^{2 x^{2} + y} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=2$ and $y=1$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=2 \\left(2\\right)^{2} + \\left(1\\right)=9.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(2,1)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(8\\right)+\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{e^{9}}{1 + e^{9}}\\right)\\cdot\\left(8\\right)+\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{4 \\left(1 + 3 e^{9}\\right)}{1 + e^{9}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTreat $x$ as constant and differentiate with respect to $y$. Apply the chain rule, and the sum and constant multiple rules where needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu&=2 x^{2} + y\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}&=1\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\ln(1+e^u)&=\\frac{1}{1+e^u}\\cdot e^u\\cdot\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(x^{2} y\\right)&=x^{2}\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u}}{e^{u} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\\\\n&=x^{2} + \\frac{e^{2 x^{2} + y}}{e^{2 x^{2} + y} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=2$ and $y=1$ after differentiating. The inner input is\n\n$$\nu_0=2 \\left(2\\right)^{2} + \\left(1\\right)=9.\n$$\n\nUsing this value in the chain factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_y(2,1)&=\\left(\\frac{e^{u_0}}{e^{u_0} + 1}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{e^{9}}{1 + e^{9}}\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)+\\left(4\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{4 + 5 e^{9}}{1 + e^{9}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese derivatives apply for all real $x,y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8eeee876-ea72-52e0-aaea-9bffd896b2ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Any value of $p$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705289} {:answer/id #uuid "b58dc4a7-a1dd-5b86-bb15-a5b7b753ba83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-4$ and $-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705295} {:answer/id #uuid "b8317694-36cb-5e28-ab58-4c2875782073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705296} {:answer/id #uuid "c410ebc6-e22a-5175-a85d-358914011d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ and $4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705297} {:answer/id #uuid "e4da1484-aa24-5f55-a40f-2a25c854f291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705300}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705295} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dcec67b-11e2-5e44-9cda-f08fcea7fea4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "08a89fa1-a53d-5ad3-97c9-5f44159c8118" :question/problem "Find all real values of $p$ for which the following matrix is singular.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ - 3 p - 4 & 4 p + 7 & - 3 p - 3 \\\\ - 5 p - 8 & 6 p + 13 & - 5 p - 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Expand along the first row, using the signs $+,-,+$. Compute the three signed contributions separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{1} & =(1)\\begin{vmatrix}4 p + 7 & - 3 p - 3 \\\\ 6 p + 13 & - 5 p - 9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1)((4 p + 7)(- 5 p - 9)-(- 3 p - 3)(6 p + 13)) \\\\  & =(1)(- 2 p^{2} - 14 p - 24) \\\\  & =- 2 p^{2} - 14 p - 24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{2} & =-(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}- 3 p - 4 & - 3 p - 3 \\\\ - 5 p - 8 & - 5 p - 9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(-2)((- 3 p - 4)(- 5 p - 9)-(- 3 p - 3)(- 5 p - 8)) \\\\  & =-(-2)(8 p + 12) \\\\  & =16 p + 24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{3} & =(2)\\begin{vmatrix}- 3 p - 4 & 4 p + 7 \\\\ - 5 p - 8 & 6 p + 13\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2)((- 3 p - 4)(6 p + 13)-(4 p + 7)(- 5 p - 8)) \\\\  & =(2)(2 p^{2} + 4 p + 4) \\\\  & =4 p^{2} + 8 p + 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the signed contributions to obtain the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =T_1+T_2+T_3 \\\\  & =2 p^{2} + 10 p + 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA singular matrix has determinant $0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 p^{2} + 10 p + 8 & =0 \\\\ 2 (p + 1) (p + 4) & =0 \\\\ p & =-4,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $p=-4$ and $p=-1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "163fe3c5-117c-50d3-b4e4-1e1ab7ad0456" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-2$ and $1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705277} {:answer/id #uuid "278180ec-78bd-5264-b491-657694531bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705279} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba48029-fb1a-5102-bac0-61297b575932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Any value of $p$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705288} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a3c725-28b3-5c88-8b27-cf22ec5c389d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-1$ and $2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705292} {:answer/id #uuid "dc554ed9-06e6-50ac-af73-df339c379c7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705298}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705277} :answer-field/id #uuid "9682fd7b-3892-5768-8819-c647568b313a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "85af9268-b852-5f3d-84a0-5ddde79250a5" :question/problem "Find all real values of $p$ for which the following matrix is singular.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}- p - 2 & - 2 p - 3 & p + 1 \\\\ 3 p + 3 & 4 p + 7 & - 3 p - 5 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Expand along the first row, using the signs $+,-,+$. Compute the three signed contributions separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{1} & =(- p - 2)\\begin{vmatrix}4 p + 7 & - 3 p - 5 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(- p - 2)((4 p + 7)(2)-(- 3 p - 5)(-2)) \\\\  & =(- p - 2)(2 p + 4) \\\\  & =- 2 p^{2} - 8 p - 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{2} & =-(- 2 p - 3)\\begin{vmatrix}3 p + 3 & - 3 p - 5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(- 2 p - 3)((3 p + 3)(2)-(- 3 p - 5)(-1)) \\\\  & =-(- 2 p - 3)(3 p + 1) \\\\  & =6 p^{2} + 11 p + 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{3} & =(p + 1)\\begin{vmatrix}3 p + 3 & 4 p + 7 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(p + 1)((3 p + 3)(-2)-(4 p + 7)(-1)) \\\\  & =(p + 1)(1 - 2 p) \\\\  & =- 2 p^{2} - p + 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the signed contributions to obtain the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =T_1+T_2+T_3 \\\\  & =2 p^{2} + 2 p - 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA singular matrix has determinant $0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 p^{2} + 2 p - 4 & =0 \\\\ 2 (p - 1) (p + 2) & =0 \\\\ p & =-2,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $p=-2$ and $p=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705286 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1541c782-1eb4-5ec0-9322-8520916fd484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Any value of $p$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705276} {:answer/id #uuid "26126201-c1f2-5abe-a041-50e7fbd8ba7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705278} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb0b957-012c-5eca-9568-4b483ff395a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-1$ and $4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705284} {:answer/id #uuid "98dfc3d5-043c-5b2e-9cc9-d096d0e3a9c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705291} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0aae61-15f0-50e9-b4b9-f63acd7eb26c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-4$ and $1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705302}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705276} :answer-field/id #uuid "31a4fc94-2a07-5a72-9b1c-b56a92072074" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "869472d3-d923-5499-8798-74bf41a36e0a" :question/problem "Find all real values of $p$ for which the following matrix is singular.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}- 2 p - 1 & p & p + 2 \\\\ 2 p - 1 & 1 - p & - p \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Expand along the first row, using the signs $+,-,+$. Compute the three signed contributions separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{1} & =(- 2 p - 1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 - p & - p \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(- 2 p - 1)((1 - p)(2)-(- p)(1)) \\\\  & =(- 2 p - 1)(2 - p) \\\\  & =2 p^{2} - 3 p - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{2} & =-(p)\\begin{vmatrix}2 p - 1 & - p \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(p)((2 p - 1)(2)-(- p)(-2)) \\\\  & =-(p)(2 p - 2) \\\\  & =- 2 p^{2} + 2 p\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{3} & =(p + 2)\\begin{vmatrix}2 p - 1 & 1 - p \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(p + 2)((2 p - 1)(1)-(1 - p)(-2)) \\\\  & =(p + 2)(1) \\\\  & =p + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the signed contributions to obtain the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =T_1+T_2+T_3 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant is $0$ for every real value of $p$. Therefore, the matrix is singular for every real value of $p$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5be13b03-5b80-500d-8b75-064858690378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705282} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ad5c23-8bd9-53aa-a17e-08702a71de72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Any value of $p$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705293} {:answer/id #uuid "b58d939e-b8d3-5fa6-a73c-19864c42aaac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-2$ and $-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705294} {:answer/id #uuid "e374ec34-e113-5dee-9eac-36b84549986f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705299} {:answer/id #uuid "ee05a336-ebc8-5ca9-8ba3-8387290a4d74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$1$ and $2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705301}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705299} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bf63112-d210-58cb-911f-c3cc2e1b3a7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8730b36b-1834-5182-9c12-0cd8c03fa744" :question/problem "Find all real values of $p$ for which the following matrix is singular.\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 p & - p - 1 & 1 - p \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 p - 5 & 4 - p & 3 - p\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Expand along the first row, using the signs $+,-,+$. Compute the three signed contributions separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{1} & =(2 p)\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 - p & 3 - p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2 p)((2)(3 - p)-(1)(4 - p)) \\\\  & =(2 p)(2 - p) \\\\  & =- 2 p^{2} + 4 p\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{2} & =-(- p - 1)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 p - 5 & 3 - p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(- p - 1)((-2)(3 - p)-(1)(2 p - 5)) \\\\  & =-(- p - 1)(-1) \\\\  & =- p - 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T_{3} & =(1 - p)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 p - 5 & 4 - p\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1 - p)((-2)(4 - p)-(2)(2 p - 5)) \\\\  & =(1 - p)(2 - 2 p) \\\\  & =2 p^{2} - 4 p + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the signed contributions to obtain the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =T_1+T_2+T_3 \\\\  & =1 - p\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA singular matrix has determinant $0$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 - p & =0 \\\\ - p & =-1 \\\\ p & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $p=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705362 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17da389e-e207-5eec-a595-f8c30612cfc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}(3\\,i-2\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705365} {:answer/id #uuid "75d95cb5-796f-50cb-84a6-7d75a92ded68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}(3\\,i+2\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705372} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e341f7-8023-50c9-a180-fb30de91afff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}(2\\,i+3\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705375} {:answer/id #uuid "c6001528-10b7-5cd5-bec3-569afa02904f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}(2\\,i-3\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705376} {:answer/id #uuid "d052daf1-65ad-5d4a-b8c0-337f8a3f1259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705377}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705375} :answer-field/id #uuid "fee10a90-1b71-5e95-817b-9c9b6eb66754" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "002466d8-9f67-5f3a-960c-fcf2af9d28bf" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=x^2+2y^2-y$ that passes through the point $(1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient at the given point is normal to the level curve. First find the partial derivatives, treating the other variable as constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2x, & f_y(x,y)&=4y-1,\\\\\n\\nabla f(x,y)&=2x\\,i+(4y-1)\\,j,\\\\\n\\nabla f(1,1)&=2\\,i+3\\,j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gradient is nonzero. Its magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\|\\nabla f(1,1)\\|=\\sqrt{2^2+3^2}=\\sqrt{13}.\n$$\n\nDivide by this magnitude to obtain a unit normal vector:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n=\\frac{\\nabla f(1,1)}{\\|\\nabla f(1,1)\\|}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}(2\\,i+3\\,j).\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal vector. Of the displayed choices, only the vector above is both parallel to the gradient and of length one."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "152ace6f-b0fb-58c0-b087-5844ad9a74a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705364} {:answer/id #uuid "28cbbd29-004c-5d46-aaba-e33ecb6d3b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(i+2\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705366} {:answer/id #uuid "36e48e4c-5ada-59fa-802d-9e7d021a9628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(2\\,i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705367} {:answer/id #uuid "d309b69d-6642-550d-b882-5f4b272210e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(i-2\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705378} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c03407-62b9-5fa4-9ed1-103030d7b171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}(i-3\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705381}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705378} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d558a8c-b99d-562f-bb20-6e7bff9be3cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "04354a68-ec3e-5c52-a17c-60e0397b6d6e" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\ln(x+2y)-xy$ that passes through the point $(2,0)$." :question/worked-solution "At the point $(2,0)$, the logarithm has positive argument $2$, so the function is differentiable nearby. A nonzero gradient there is normal to the level curve.\n\nDifferentiate before substituting the coordinates. The chain rule for the logarithm and the derivatives of $-xy$ give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{1}{x+2y}-y,\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\frac{2}{x+2y}-x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(2,0)&=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}-0\\right)i+\\left(\\frac{2}{2}-2\\right)j\\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{2}i-j,\\\\\n\\|\\nabla f(2,0)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2+(-1)^2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNormalize this nonzero vector:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n=\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}i-j}{\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(i-2\\,j).\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal vector. Omitting the derivatives of $-xy$ gives a different direction; keeping the gradient without normalization gives the wrong length."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dc6a29c-43ee-52f9-a631-1d67e5e73098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705368} {:answer/id #uuid "4a999d6e-a238-5803-87d0-5af6bda20a20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\left((1+\\sqrt{3})\\,i+(\\sqrt{3}-1)\\,j\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705369} {:answer/id #uuid "5c88f6d1-97b4-5909-bb1a-1a360888204f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\left((\\sqrt{3}-1)\\,i+(1+\\sqrt{3})\\,j\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705370} {:answer/id #uuid "96872924-94c3-547b-8e31-d584c5da7410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\left((1+\\sqrt{3})\\,i-(\\sqrt{3}-1)\\,j\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705374} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb17987-44a3-5b1d-96a9-c158aeb0288c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\left((1+\\sqrt{3})\\,i+(\\sqrt{3}-1)\\,j\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705380}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705380} :answer-field/id #uuid "df236f0e-ea0c-5c66-b1e0-4e00ae187085" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7e80eb4c-cde7-5721-a8e4-918dc716189e" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=e^{x-y}\\sin(x+y)$ that passes through the point $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "The function is differentiable everywhere. To find a normal vector to its level curve, compute the gradient at the specified point. Apply the product and chain rules to both partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=e^{x-y}\\sin(x+y)+e^{x-y}\\cos(x+y),\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=-e^{x-y}\\sin(x+y)+e^{x-y}\\cos(x+y).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0\\right)$, use $\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{6}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{6}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0\\right)=\\frac{e^{\\pi/6}}{2}\\left((1+\\sqrt{3})\\,i+(\\sqrt{3}-1)\\,j\\right).\n$$\n\nThe common factor is positive, and this vector is nonzero. Its magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left\\|\\nabla f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{6},0\\right)\\right\\|\n&=\\frac{e^{\\pi/6}}{2}\\sqrt{(1+\\sqrt{3})^2+(\\sqrt{3}-1)^2}\\\\\n&=\\frac{e^{\\pi/6}}{2}\\sqrt{(4+2\\sqrt{3})+(4-2\\sqrt{3})}\\\\\n&=e^{\\pi/6}\\sqrt{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDivide the gradient by its magnitude. The positive exponential factor cancels:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\left((1+\\sqrt{3})\\,i+(\\sqrt{3}-1)\\,j\\right).\n$$\n\nIts negative is also a unit normal vector. Both product-rule terms matter: differentiating only the sine factor would incorrectly give the direction $i+j$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705395 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c658c51-237b-5efe-8fc0-9f3959a4dca9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5x+y-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705384} {:answer/id #uuid "59a1d040-80f2-55b4-8cfd-a520d8ed84a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5x-y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705388} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9bc0a9-f794-5c30-8d61-9ec611ae2fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x+5y-9=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705389} {:answer/id #uuid "e54823c4-4594-526d-abbb-68162cd82c90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5x+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705401} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4af41d-2b8f-51ec-9f64-78cd20519446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+5y-11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705402}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705384} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb3d09a5-3a3d-5942-a325-6d01ce9870c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a9bdc970-6475-55ce-b187-e33e9fd731c0" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2xy=x^2+3y^2-9.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at $(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Bring the terms to one side:\n\n$$\nx^2+3y^2-2xy-9=0.\n$$\n\nSet $f(x,y)=x^2+3y^2-2xy-9.$ The curve is the level set $f(x,y)=0.$ Its gradient at $(1,2)$ supplies a normal direction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2x-2y, & f_y(x,y)&=6y-2x,\\\\\n\\nabla f(1,2)&=\\langle 2-4,12-2\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle -2,10\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line through $(1,2)$ parallel to $\\langle -2,10\\rangle$ has equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}&=\\frac{y-2}{10},\\\\\n10(x-1)&=-2(y-2),\\\\\n5x+y-7&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the normal line is $5x+y-7=0.$ Its slope is $-5.$ In contrast, the tangent line has slope $1/5$ and equation $-x+5y-9=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705399 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "500e7dde-a901-53fc-abd4-dafe09343260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705387} {:answer/id #uuid "73e8b235-ab6c-5975-b4e5-98e0205a58a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705390} {:answer/id #uuid "743ae627-2d67-59c1-93ea-852d84f4099a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705391} {:answer/id #uuid "9179a911-569b-5481-ab4d-3a985c529a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705392} {:answer/id #uuid "c403a129-79d6-552a-a7a1-6534f6f8ad23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705398}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705390} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2e6df7a-6f3a-56c7-94c3-4738fe030f80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dbb425aa-e7df-595c-96e2-14f5589508db" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-1)^2}{4}+\\frac{(y+2)^2}{9}=1.\n$$\n\nFind an equation of the normal line to the curve at $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Set\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{(x-1)^2}{4}+\\frac{(y+2)^2}{9}.\n$$\n\nThe curve is the level set $f(x,y)=1.$ Compute its gradient:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x,y)&=\\left\\langle \\frac{x-1}{2},\\frac{2(y+2)}{9}\\right\\rangle,\\\\\n\\nabla f(1,1)&=\\left\\langle 0,\\frac{2}{3}\\right\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero and points vertically. The normal line is therefore the vertical line through $(1,1):$\n\n$$\nx=1.\n$$\n\nWe can also use the relation $b(x-x_0)=a(y-y_0)$ for a line parallel to $\\langle a,b\\rangle:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{3}(x-1)&=0(y-1),\\\\\nx&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis form handles the zero first component without division by zero. The line $y=1$ is the horizontal tangent, whereas $x=0$ has the normal direction but misses the given point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705400 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9bb1b9-f68f-5a9b-a74d-f8bd6600397e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705383} {:answer/id #uuid "4be12e8b-eb4f-5163-ba2a-08c9ccaaca10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705385} {:answer/id #uuid "4d48e2b0-1acc-522b-93fd-cc56424d63ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705386} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f99ff4-7453-5f62-8024-044cbd739680" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2y+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705394} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a0d2c7-5922-5fbb-9b85-23340331a12c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705396}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705385} :answer-field/id #uuid "edac0285-1ef8-5bf6-bfcc-3c4a54ab4643" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "df2ae96b-3155-534b-b41a-f5f00d3de846" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^2+2y^2=3.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at $(1,1).$\n\nUse $f(x,y)=x^2+2y^2.$ The vector $\\nabla f(1,1)$ gives the direction of the normal line. For a direction vector $\\langle a,b\\rangle,$ a line through $(x_0,y_0)$ satisfies $b(x-x_0)=a(y-y_0).$" :question/worked-solution "The curve is the level curve $f(x,y)=3.$ Compute the partial derivatives, then evaluate the gradient at the given point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=2x, & f_y(x,y)&=4y,\\\\\n\\nabla f(1,1)&=\\langle 2,4\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this gradient is nonzero, it is a direction vector for the normal line. Using $a=2,$ $b=4,$ and $(x_0,y_0)=(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4(x-1)&=2(y-1),\\\\\n2(x-1)&=y-1,\\\\\n2x-y-1&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the normal line is $2x-y-1=0.$ It passes through $(1,1)$ and has slope $2,$ the slope of the vector $\\langle 2,4\\rangle.$ The equation $x+2y-3=0$ instead describes the tangent line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19cd1a21-237e-5620-8fe4-9b21cfce8ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705413} {:answer/id #uuid "20a3f9bd-3718-5152-8882-e8318ff20bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\,\\mathbf i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705414} {:answer/id #uuid "2ec5f88a-4669-5b8b-8d71-f86c822fc244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705416} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ca6696-3a76-5491-a5b9-29afba022bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705426} {:answer/id #uuid "dbaa7930-88db-542d-8ad0-fd3145984f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705430}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705414} :answer-field/id #uuid "de157c71-a693-502d-8f11-22b273c62407" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "22105716-8049-526d-af73-0112b2a8104e" :question/problem "Let\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=(x^2+y^2+z^2-1)^2.\n$$\n\nFind a nonzero normal vector at $P=(1,0,0)$ to the level surface of $f$ that passes through $P$." :question/worked-solution "First determine which level surface passes through $P$. Since $f(1,0,0)=0$, its equation is\n\n$$\n(x^2+y^2+z^2-1)^2=0.\n$$\n\nA real number has square zero exactly when it is zero, so this is the sphere\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2+z^2=1.\n$$\n\nDirect differentiation of the supplied function gives\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=4(x^2+y^2+z^2-1)(x\\,\\mathbf i+y\\,\\mathbf j+z\\,\\mathbf k),\n$$\n\nso $\\nabla f(P)=\\mathbf 0$. The zero vector has no direction and is not a normal vector. A zero gradient means that this defining function does not supply a normal through the nonzero-gradient rule; it does not mean that the surface has no normal.\n\nUse the equivalent defining function $g(x,y,z)=x^2+y^2+z^2-1$ for the same surface. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla g(x,y,z)&=2x\\,\\mathbf i+2y\\,\\mathbf j+2z\\,\\mathbf k,\\\\\n\\nabla g(1,0,0)&=2\\,\\mathbf i\\ne\\mathbf 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus every nonzero multiple of $\\mathbf i$ is normal here. The listed vector $-3\\,\\mathbf i=-\\frac{3}{2}\\nabla g(P)$ is a normal vector. The vectors $\\mathbf j$ and $\\mathbf k$ are tangent directions, and $\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j$ is not parallel to the normal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d0b2c4-a836-558c-b718-ec19ea42e45e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j+4\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705411} {:answer/id #uuid "09efac3d-3ce2-5f75-b6f7-e6b4dcb4e244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j+4\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705412} {:answer/id #uuid "66a5aae7-ae1e-58d1-9b7c-6efef7a055b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j+4\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705420} {:answer/id #uuid "965b7d8d-91a5-5ddf-891b-38204c66c650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j+2\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705425} {:answer/id #uuid "d9425bd3-306b-5c60-8690-26467565e05f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j+2\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705429}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705411} :answer-field/id #uuid "750b4f81-fa4e-53ac-992d-a6fe5de8a4f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "601fb8da-e38a-505e-a8fd-8e492feae90a" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x^2+xy+z^2$, find a vector perpendicular to the level surface that passes through $P=(1,-1,2)$." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero gradient at $P$ is a normal vector to the level surface through $P$. First compute the three partial derivatives, treating the other variables as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x&=2x+y, & f_y&=x, & f_z&=2z. \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=(2x+y)\\,\\mathbf i+x\\,\\mathbf j+2z\\,\\mathbf k,\\\\\n\\nabla f(1,-1,2)&=(2(1)-1)\\,\\mathbf i+1\\,\\mathbf j+2(2)\\,\\mathbf k\\\\\n&=\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j+4\\,\\mathbf k.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis vector is nonzero, so $\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j+4\\,\\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the level surface at $P$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55650769-9ee5-5f99-bd26-39c4165f1ec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705417} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc270e9-c3f1-5c79-8f28-1195612a588d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j+2\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705418} {:answer/id #uuid "8528f04f-6314-5c5b-a117-5852fc9cd10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705423} {:answer/id #uuid "8f90ff14-29e3-5d55-8a56-907bec938064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705424} {:answer/id #uuid "c396caa9-7f3f-53de-8b4d-301451ca8430" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,\\mathbf i-2\\,\\mathbf k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705427}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705423} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9cb0047-a21e-5f79-932e-323b5fae1748" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "84acf384-aaa4-5a09-8118-a9e03c591acc" :question/problem "Find a nonzero normal vector to the surface\n\n$$\nx^2+xy+z^2=y+2\n$$\n\nat $P=(1,1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Write the surface as a level surface by moving all terms to one side:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=x^2+xy+z^2-y-2.\n$$\n\nThe surface has equation $F(x,y,z)=0$. The point lies on it because $F(1,1,1)=1+1+1-1-2=0$. Compute the gradient of the whole defining function, including the term $-y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla F(x,y,z)&=(2x+y)\\,\\mathbf i+(x-1)\\,\\mathbf j+2z\\,\\mathbf k,\\\\\n\\nabla F(1,1,1)&=3\\,\\mathbf i+0\\,\\mathbf j+2\\,\\mathbf k.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero. Every nonzero scalar multiple of it is also normal to the surface. In particular,\n\n$$\n-2\\nabla F(1,1,1)=-6\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-6\\,\\mathbf i-4\\,\\mathbf k$ is the listed normal vector. Differentiating only the left side would incorrectly leave a $\\mathbf j$ component."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "743ec730-80fc-5ce7-a2cf-125e7c91dd9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 1,-1,3\\rangle+t\\langle 2,-4,-1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705442} {:answer/id #uuid "a517610a-7092-56e9-9069-f788376dfa9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 1,1,3\\rangle+t\\langle 2,-4,-1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705446} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc26234-6276-5be0-8cd9-9807b6f92dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 1,-1,3\\rangle+t\\langle 2,4,-1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705449} {:answer/id #uuid "eada29fb-5ff5-519c-b0f2-e4fe0598afef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 1,-1,3\\rangle+t\\langle 1,-1,3\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705450} {:answer/id #uuid "f64a29ba-d1b8-5c2b-9bc8-86e1fafb3301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 2,-4,-1\\rangle+t\\langle 1,-1,3\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705451}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705442} :answer-field/id #uuid "f944ed9a-983f-5157-b6d5-ed8e51fa24d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "00efc182-3086-5015-924c-f2b001535013" :question/problem "The surface $x^2+2y^2-z=0$ contains the point $P(1,-1,3)$. For $F(x,y,z)=x^2+2y^2-z$, you are given\n\n$$\n\\nabla F(1,-1,3)=\\langle 2,-4,-1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWhich vector equation describes the normal line to the surface at $P$? Use $t\\in\\mathbb R$, with $t=0$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "A nonzero gradient of a level function is perpendicular to its level surface. Thus $\\langle 2,-4,-1\\rangle$ is a direction vector for the normal line.\n\nThe position vector of $P$ is $\\langle 1,-1,3\\rangle$. A line through $P$ with direction vector $\\mathbf n$ has equation $\\mathbf r=\\mathbf p+t\\mathbf n$, so\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle 1,-1,3\\rangle+t\\langle 2,-4,-1\\rangle,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$ this gives $P$. The point supplies the constant vector, and the gradient supplies the direction vector; they have different roles."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "156dc22d-cc37-532c-9500-ac991785718e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-1=y=\\frac{z-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705435} {:answer/id #uuid "3c51c929-f0e5-55eb-9d62-988856bed197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-x=y=\\frac{z-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705436} {:answer/id #uuid "4a364be2-b8ff-5960-8973-505e2de79455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-x=y=\\frac{z+1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705438} {:answer/id #uuid "56ff45ab-eca0-5f41-8fc4-27961ca91dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1-x}{2}=y=\\frac{z-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705439} {:answer/id #uuid "9036ed35-91ce-5701-8123-7565371653c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-x=y=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705444}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705436} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f3146a4-ef83-5347-b77e-fc0bd6ca8a92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a98dbcde-c4e6-5a4a-a4ea-5878c9a0247f" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $e^{xy}+z^2=x+1$ at the point $(1,0,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Move the variable term $x$ to the left and set\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=e^{xy}+z^2-x.\n$$\n\nThe surface is the level surface $F=1$. Using the chain rule for $e^{xy}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\nabla F(x,y,z)=\\langle ye^{xy}-1,\\ xe^{xy},\\ 2z\\rangle.\n$$\n\nAt $P=(1,0,1)$, we have $e^{xy}=e^0=1$, so\n\n$$\n\\nabla F(1,0,1)=\\langle 0(1)-1,\\ 1(1),\\ 2(1)\\rangle=\\langle -1,1,2\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe gradient is nonzero and supplies the direction of the normal line. Thus a vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle 1,0,1\\rangle+t\\langle -1,1,2\\rangle,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nWriting $x=1-t$, $y=t$, and $z=1+2t$ and eliminating $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-1}=\\frac{y-0}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required equation is\n\n$$\n1-x=y=\\frac{z-1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "133a810d-05b0-5a9d-86d7-e97126fd9828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 1,1,2\\rangle+t\\langle 1,-3,-1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705434} {:answer/id #uuid "41fd01f3-fc2f-5f97-b619-744a2fcabb24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 1,-3,-1\\rangle+t\\langle 1,-1,2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705437} {:answer/id #uuid "6a688555-2bbe-5b3c-a483-bcb1751f9117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 1,-1,2\\rangle+t\\langle 2,-4,-1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705441} {:answer/id #uuid "7b75a489-586a-56ff-8209-c81084075d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 1,-1,2\\rangle+t\\langle 1,-3,-1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705443} {:answer/id #uuid "944be60b-6119-5a2b-8611-7067a35ea71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\mathbf r=\\langle 1,-1,2\\rangle+t\\langle 1,-3,1\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705445}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705443} :answer-field/id #uuid "05e48d41-3060-5b36-9319-07e665ce9501" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b829ed18-b8df-518d-ad80-543aba34a3d5" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $z=x^2+xy+2y^2$ at $P(1,-1,2)$. Use $t\\in\\mathbb R$, with $t=0$ at $P$." :question/worked-solution "Bring all variable terms to one side and define\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=x^2+xy+2y^2-z.\n$$\n\nThe surface is the level surface $F=0$. Its gradient is\n\n$$\n\\nabla F(x,y,z)=\\langle 2x+y,\\ x+4y,\\ -1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nEvaluate at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla F(1,-1,2)&=\\langle 2(1)-1,\\ 1+4(-1),\\ -1\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 1,-3,-1\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis nonzero vector is normal to the surface. Using $P$ as the base point, the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r=\\langle 1,-1,2\\rangle+t\\langle 1,-3,-1\\rangle,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nThe constant vector gives $P$ at $t=0$, while the coefficient of $t$ gives the normal direction. Multiplying all components of that direction by the same nonzero scalar would describe the same line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705474 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06004c2d-8a38-5a23-b417-512129f1db37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 14,\\,8\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705466} {:answer/id #uuid "ad393726-9a25-589f-a390-3fbfc5d979f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 14,\\,6\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705484} {:answer/id #uuid "bbb12820-9ce8-5a28-bf56-387d64b76240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 2,\\,2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705489} {:answer/id #uuid "c69c095b-9df4-5f3f-9c7d-89e796345271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 12,\\,2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705491} {:answer/id #uuid "db12b336-e362-5518-bfa9-05ff5279746c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 10,\\,-2\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705493}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705484} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb475826-153f-5a33-8cf9-24d277b4bd9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705488}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6d8ff01a-4a41-5901-a733-09b6e6e36bc4" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\nabla f(x,y)$ at $(2,1)$ if $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} + 2 x y + 2 y^{2} - 2 y$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to one coordinate at a time, holding the other constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=6 x+2 y+0+0 \\\\ &=6 x + 2 y \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=0+2 x+4 y-2 \\\\ &=2 x + 4 y - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partial derivatives in order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=(6 x + 2 y)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+(2 x + 4 y - 2)\\,\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(2,1)&=6 \\left(2\\right) + 2 \\left(1\\right) \\\\ &=14 \\\\ f_y(2,1)&=2 \\left(2\\right) + 4 \\left(1\\right) - 2 \\\\ &=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(2,1)=\\langle 14,\\,6\\rangle$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4978112b-47db-5c25-91bf-0ab7a9d2df94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 3,\\,18\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705470} {:answer/id #uuid "8084856b-b49b-5343-b1c4-2611e011cf41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{1}{2},\\,27\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705478} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea1e4d0-6128-5f75-8e30-07de9a8d6e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 0,\\,6\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705481} {:answer/id #uuid "beedadae-bec0-5757-8bd6-c99812507791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{3}{2},\\,18\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705490} {:answer/id #uuid "cbeb1ed2-978b-5a0a-ad43-86994ee2afd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle - \\frac{3}{2},\\,18\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705492}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705490} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d642ab2-4e5b-513f-9d50-b2471fa35011" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "714cdd5e-3a09-50b7-a250-b5aa4f10711d" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\nabla f(x,y)$ at $(2,3)$ if $f(x,y)=y^{2} \\sqrt{x + 7}$. Work on the domain $x + 7>0$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to one coordinate at a time, holding the other constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=y^{2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x + 7}} \\\\ &=\\frac{y^{2}}{2 \\sqrt{x + 7}} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=2 y\\sqrt{x + 7} \\\\ &=2 y \\sqrt{x + 7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partial derivatives in order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{y^{2}}{2 \\sqrt{x + 7}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+(2 y \\sqrt{x + 7})\\,\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(2,3)&=\\frac{\\left(3\\right)^{2}}{2 \\sqrt{\\left(2\\right) + 7}} \\\\ &=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ f_y(2,3)&=2 \\left(3\\right) \\sqrt{\\left(2\\right) + 7} \\\\ &=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(2,3)=\\langle \\frac{3}{2},\\,18\\rangle$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76c86401-8a33-5a3c-bc27-42e8705a4f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle - y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - \\frac{2 y^{4}}{x},\\,- x \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - 8 y^{3} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705476} {:answer/id #uuid "a898a97b-fdd9-5db4-aef0-476533a527ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle - \\frac{2 y^{4}}{x},\\,- x \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705482} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c47f85-6f12-52ed-a554-4024e4e81245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle - x \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - 8 y^{3} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)},\\,- y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - \\frac{2 y^{4}}{x}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705487} {:answer/id #uuid "f356aebf-496b-5929-8c2f-5bc99b401233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle - y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - \\frac{8 y^{4}}{x},\\,- x \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - 2 y^{3} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705499} {:answer/id #uuid "fecbc32e-2fa0-5870-8739-b8d28df1e47b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle - y \\sin{\\left(x y \\right)} - \\frac{2 y^{4}}{x},\\,- x \\sin{\\left(x y \\right)} - 8 y^{3} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705500}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705476} :answer-field/id #uuid "af1b7529-201f-5bd9-9d6f-cd96f02b1d7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7c4cf1b0-f488-525a-b216-87968565cf72" :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=- 2 y^{4} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(x y \\right)}$. Work on the domain $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to one coordinate at a time, holding the other constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=- y \\cos{\\left(y x \\right)}- 2 y^{4}\\cdot\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ &=- y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - \\frac{2 y^{4}}{x} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=- x \\cos{\\left(y x \\right)}- 8 y^{3} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} \\\\ &=- x \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - 8 y^{3} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partial derivatives in order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=(- y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - \\frac{2 y^{4}}{x})\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+(- x \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - 8 y^{3} \\ln{\\left(x \\right)})\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705486 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45e33883-a613-550b-9020-04c6a531e49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{6 y}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}},\\,\\frac{6 x}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705469} {:answer/id #uuid "613f7470-6d98-5b16-a933-965545eb0f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle - \\frac{6 y}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}},\\,- \\frac{6 x}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705472} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae019e2-aba5-59e2-b14e-c17fce88f1f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle - \\frac{6 x}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}},\\,\\frac{6 y}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705479} {:answer/id #uuid "dc879a3c-b380-517d-9b2a-a68064a14337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{3}{x - y},\\,\\frac{3}{x - y}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705494} {:answer/id #uuid "e74637f0-0f54-5691-af18-2f8bad670be2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle - \\frac{6 y}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}},\\,\\frac{6 x}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705497}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705497} :answer-field/id #uuid "6153c867-2d56-5a9e-98cf-0e7c695ca332" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b2371933-44d9-59c6-b9e7-e89954dad96a" :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=\\frac{- 3 x - 3 y}{- x + y}$. Work on the domain $y - x\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Apply the quotient rule to each partial. The denominator-derivative contribution is subtracted in both components.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=\\frac{(-3)(y - x)-(- 3 y - 3 x)(-1)}{(y - x)^2} \\\\ &=- \\frac{6 y}{\\left(- x + y\\right)^{2}} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{(-3)(y - x)-(- 3 y - 3 x)\\cdot1}{(y - x)^2} \\\\ &=\\frac{6 x}{\\left(- x + y\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partial derivatives in order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=\\left(- \\frac{6 y}{\\left(- x + y\\right)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(\\frac{6 x}{\\left(- x + y\\right)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705498 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "408a959d-c696-54ef-851f-aae74b7501f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle -1,\\,4\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705468} {:answer/id #uuid "4e54f689-3b36-5591-bc75-0762226bc5f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle -1,\\,0\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705471} {:answer/id #uuid "acf41e41-f9f7-5b5f-a2dd-4a484bd67aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle -1,\\,-4\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705483} {:answer/id #uuid "decce870-6d71-59f9-b30c-02f8adc78f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 1,\\,0\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705495} {:answer/id #uuid "e35c2d36-273b-54c3-8fc2-09efdc69a504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle 1,\\,-4\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705496}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705471} :answer-field/id #uuid "34f3c586-2e71-51dd-9241-a48979690dbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f13cb373-475b-56a8-b37c-065acafc577f" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\nabla f(x,y)$ at $(-2,1)$ if $f(x,y)=e^{- x y - y^{2} - 1}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate with respect to one coordinate at a time, holding the other constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=- ye^{- y^{2} - y x - 1} \\\\ &=- y e^{- x y - y^{2} - 1} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=(- 2 y - x)e^{- y^{2} - y x - 1} \\\\ &=\\left(- x - 2 y\\right) e^{- x y - y^{2} - 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partial derivatives in order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=(- y e^{- x y - y^{2} - 1})\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+(\\left(- x - 2 y\\right) e^{- x y - y^{2} - 1})\\,\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(-2,1)&=- \\left(1\\right) e^{- \\left(-2\\right) \\left(1\\right) - \\left(1\\right)^{2} - 1} \\\\ &=-1 \\\\ f_y(-2,1)&=\\left(- \\left(-2\\right) - 2 \\left(1\\right)\\right) e^{- \\left(-2\\right) \\left(1\\right) - \\left(1\\right)^{2} - 1} \\\\ &=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(-2,1)=\\langle -1,\\,0\\rangle$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e8f709c-8164-5ec3-8ee5-d04bf94b669a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{y \\left(3 x^{2} - 6 x y + 2 y\\right)}{x - y},\\,\\frac{3 x^{3} - 4 x y + 2 y^{2}}{x - y}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705509} {:answer/id #uuid "a07b5807-5ff5-52be-8fdc-666efb3dcbf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{y \\left(3 x^{2} - 6 x y + 2 y\\right)}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}},\\,\\frac{3 x^{3} - 6 x^{2} y - 4 x y + 6 y^{2}}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705514} {:answer/id #uuid "a54669e3-6f2a-5fcb-a8e0-c5b2df5fe69d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{6 x y}{x - y},\\,\\frac{3 x^{2} - 4 y}{x - y}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705516} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a64dd7-1356-59a9-874a-5809a7440157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{y \\left(9 x^{2} - 6 x y - 2 y\\right)}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}},\\,\\frac{3 x^{3} - 6 x^{2} y - 4 x y + 6 y^{2}}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705520} {:answer/id #uuid "fcba2d9f-63de-53e3-9830-59eae900dd19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{y \\left(3 x^{2} - 6 x y + 2 y\\right)}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}},\\,\\frac{3 x^{3} - 4 x y + 2 y^{2}}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705529}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705529} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fb67bdc-44ae-5f47-a171-8e49455a4a7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8b918634-29d4-5eb1-b727-4a1354dcb20f" :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=\\frac{3 x^{2} y - 2 y^{2}}{x - y}$. Work on the domain $x - y\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Apply the quotient rule to each partial. The denominator-derivative contribution is subtracted in both components.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=\\frac{(6 x y)(x - y)-(3 x^{2} y - 2 y^{2})\\cdot1}{(x - y)^2} \\\\ &=\\frac{y \\left(3 x^{2} - 6 x y + 2 y\\right)}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{(3 x^{2} - 4 y)(x - y)-(3 x^{2} y - 2 y^{2})(-1)}{(x - y)^2} \\\\ &=\\frac{3 x^{3} - 4 x y + 2 y^{2}}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partial derivatives in order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{y \\left(3 x^{2} - 6 x y + 2 y\\right)}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(\\frac{3 x^{3} - 4 x y + 2 y^{2}}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e7de28b-77e2-5557-9d58-bb153d353295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{2 \\left(x^{2} + 2 x y - y^{3}\\right)}{\\left(x + y\\right)^{2}},\\,- \\frac{2 x \\left(2 x y - x + 3 y^{2}\\right)}{\\left(x + y\\right)^{2}}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705505} {:answer/id #uuid "2c57e07f-2377-5dd7-912d-0ffe9461d221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{2 \\left(x^{2} + 2 x y - y^{3}\\right)}{\\left(x + y\\right)^{2}},\\,- \\frac{2 x \\left(2 x y + x + y^{2}\\right)}{\\left(x + y\\right)^{2}}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705508} {:answer/id #uuid "a9521c18-5f2d-59d3-9db8-1d07c6b59357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{2 \\left(2 x - y^{2}\\right)}{x + y},\\,- \\frac{4 x y}{x + y}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705517} {:answer/id #uuid "efbc373f-adbf-5d48-93af-100f2262c7f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{2 \\left(3 x^{2} - 2 x y^{2} + 2 x y - y^{3}\\right)}{\\left(x + y\\right)^{2}},\\,- \\frac{2 x \\left(2 x y + x + y^{2}\\right)}{\\left(x + y\\right)^{2}}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705524} {:answer/id #uuid "f5dde755-11d2-53d8-98f3-7f2fcd02a2ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{2 \\left(x^{2} + 2 x y - y^{3}\\right)}{x + y},\\,- \\frac{2 x \\left(2 x y + x + y^{2}\\right)}{x + y}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705527}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705508} :answer-field/id #uuid "81472deb-f1e5-55a9-8482-9e7ff500e7f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a2b22e35-a9a9-57c8-bfef-c5032a2949c5" :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=\\frac{2 x^{2} - 2 x y^{2}}{x + y}$. Work on the domain $y + x\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Apply the quotient rule to each partial. The denominator-derivative contribution is subtracted in both components.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=\\frac{(- 2 y^{2} + 4 x)(y + x)-(- 2 y^{2} x + 2 x^{2})(1)}{(y + x)^2} \\\\ &=- \\frac{2 \\left(- x^{2} - 2 x y + y^{3}\\right)}{\\left(x + y\\right)^{2}} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{(- 4 y x)(y + x)-(- 2 y^{2} x + 2 x^{2})\\cdot1}{(y + x)^2} \\\\ &=- \\frac{2 x \\left(2 x y + x + y^{2}\\right)}{\\left(x + y\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partial derivatives in order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=\\left(- \\frac{2 \\left(- x^{2} - 2 x y + y^{3}\\right)}{\\left(x + y\\right)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(- \\frac{2 x \\left(2 x y + x + y^{2}\\right)}{\\left(x + y\\right)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705522 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04754151-1297-532a-bc45-003f25d8212b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{y \\left(2 x^{2} + 8 x y - 3 y\\right)}{x + 2 y},\\,\\frac{2 \\left(x^{3} + 3 x y + 3 y^{2}\\right)}{x + 2 y}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705502} {:answer/id #uuid "068e4c7b-3140-501b-914c-88d12f075ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{y \\left(6 x^{2} + 8 x y + 3 y\\right)}{\\left(x + 2 y\\right)^{2}},\\,\\frac{2 \\left(x^{3} + 3 x y + 3 y^{2}\\right)}{\\left(x + 2 y\\right)^{2}}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705503} {:answer/id #uuid "2233ac3a-f52a-52ea-a1a8-339942da1527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{y \\left(2 x^{2} + 8 x y - 3 y\\right)}{\\left(x + 2 y\\right)^{2}},\\,\\frac{2 \\left(x^{3} + 3 x y + 3 y^{2}\\right)}{\\left(x + 2 y\\right)^{2}}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705506} {:answer/id #uuid "af732e96-19a1-5a39-a70d-23ff5ceeaa71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle \\frac{2 \\left(x^{3} + 3 x y + 3 y^{2}\\right)}{\\left(x + 2 y\\right)^{2}},\\,\\frac{y \\left(2 x^{2} + 8 x y - 3 y\\right)}{\\left(x + 2 y\\right)^{2}}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705519} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d3432c-7094-50a1-95f0-be64829dae04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle - \\frac{y \\left(2 x^{2} + 8 x y - 3 y\\right)}{\\left(x + 2 y\\right)^{2}},\\,- \\frac{2 \\left(x^{3} + 3 x y + 3 y^{2}\\right)}{\\left(x + 2 y\\right)^{2}}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705521}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705506} :answer-field/id #uuid "086a3688-cf9e-5320-ab43-1fdd693632c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dbdbfa92-edbb-59f3-bf52-4484fda28f8a" :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=\\frac{2 x^{2} y + 3 y^{2}}{x + 2 y}$. Work on the domain $x + 2 y\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Apply the quotient rule to each partial. The denominator-derivative contribution is subtracted in both components.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=\\frac{(4 x y)(x + 2 y)-(2 x^{2} y + 3 y^{2})\\cdot1}{(x + 2 y)^2} \\\\ &=\\frac{y \\left(2 x^{2} + 8 x y - 3 y\\right)}{\\left(x + 2 y\\right)^{2}} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{(2 x^{2} + 6 y)(x + 2 y)-(2 x^{2} y + 3 y^{2})(2)}{(x + 2 y)^2} \\\\ &=\\frac{2 \\left(x^{3} + 3 x y + 3 y^{2}\\right)}{\\left(x + 2 y\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partial derivatives in order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=\\left(\\frac{y \\left(2 x^{2} + 8 x y - 3 y\\right)}{\\left(x + 2 y\\right)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(\\frac{2 \\left(x^{3} + 3 x y + 3 y^{2}\\right)}{\\left(x + 2 y\\right)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26bf8170-042b-558e-ae81-26025718eca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle - \\frac{y \\left(x^{2} - 2 x y - y\\right)}{x - y},\\,- \\frac{x^{3} + 2 x y - y^{2}}{x - y}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705507} {:answer/id #uuid "42c7b5e7-a0f5-56e8-8507-fd99c2145a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle - \\frac{y \\left(x^{2} - 2 x y - y\\right)}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}},\\,- \\frac{x^{3} + 2 x y - y^{2}}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705510} {:answer/id #uuid "af20a679-cff3-5ba6-8a64-f20cd197aa44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle - \\frac{y \\left(3 x^{2} - 2 x y + y\\right)}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}},\\,- \\frac{x^{3} + 2 x y - y^{2}}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705518} {:answer/id #uuid "eb50de88-71d5-5241-a5b3-62938b1f1c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle - \\frac{2 x y}{x - y},\\,- \\frac{x^{2} + 2 y}{x - y}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705523} {:answer/id #uuid "f771790c-81e9-5e6b-81b9-b36460fcd440" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\langle - \\frac{y \\left(x^{2} - 2 x y - y\\right)}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}},\\,- \\frac{x^{3} - 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y - 3 y^{2}}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\rangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705528}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705510} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3fdf4ac-41bf-56bc-8acd-1a950ab7e056" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186705526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f2f1d2a2-96f9-5c70-a8a4-a50ab2a7542e" :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y)$ if $f(x,y)=\\frac{- x^{2} y - y^{2}}{x - y}$. Work on the domain $x - y\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "Apply the quotient rule to each partial. The denominator-derivative contribution is subtracted in both components.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=\\frac{(- 2 x y)(x - y)-(- x^{2} y - y^{2})\\cdot1}{(x - y)^2} \\\\ &=- \\frac{y \\left(x^{2} - 2 x y - y\\right)}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}} \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{(- x^{2} - 2 y)(x - y)-(- x^{2} y - y^{2})(-1)}{(x - y)^2} \\\\ &=- \\frac{x^{3} + 2 x y - y^{2}}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the partial derivatives in order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y)&=\\left(- \\frac{y \\left(x^{2} - 2 x y - y\\right)}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(- \\frac{x^{3} + 2 x y - y^{2}}{\\left(x - y\\right)^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
